The next step is that we should find a way to compare the proximity count and the gps distance. I tried several ways to do that. And find this way is the best. 


As shown in this figure, we divided the distance into various ranges, from 10m to 30m. Then, we count how many pairs of the distance fall into the range. And divided this value by the total number of pairs. 

And this value should have the same trend with the proximity logger data. 

Here, we have two hyperparameters to set. 

The first one is the aggregation window. I tested aggregation window from 30min to 4 hours in 30-minutes increments. Longer interval results in more stable data. 

The second one is the gps close range. I tested the range from 10ms to 50m in 5 meters increments.

I found that the range of 15m is reasonable. According to the previous analysis, our new gps devices have the accuracy error of approximitely 10m. So if  the two sheep are close together, 15m is reasonable setting. 

Here is the time series of proximity rate and the gps close ratio. As we can see, the two values should have a high correlation. 

